# Line-ending and encoding policy for release assets.
#
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 (the default `powershell.exe`) decodes a script using the
# ANSI code page unless the file carries a UTF-8 BOM, so release scripts are stored
# with a BOM and must keep CRLF in the working tree on *every* platform. Without
# `eol=crlf`, git normalises the line endings to LF on checkout elsewhere and the
# shipped launcher no longer matches what Windows users receive.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell/CI helpers stay LF everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party browser bundles are byte-exact artifacts: `VENDOR.json`
# records their SHA256 and `tests/test_web_workbench.py` recomputes it. Any
# end-of-line conversion on checkout (notably `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows)
# would change the bytes and make that integrity check meaningless.
aivoicebench/static/vendor/** -text
